# context prints the format, layers, registry, and rules for an agent.
cd $EXAMPLES/refund
exits 0 sv context
cmp stdout $WORK/refund.txt
! stderr .

# With an empty registry, context says so.
cd $WORK/empty
exits 0 sv context
stdout '^## Registered services\n\(none\)$'

# context lists a registered service that has no valid layer on its own line.
# The registry rules report that service.
cd $WORK/numberlayer
exits 0 sv context
stdout '^- manager: OrderManager$'
stdout '^- no valid layer: NumberEngine, StringAccessor$'

# context lists the limits of the registry.
cd $WORK/limits
exits 0 sv context
stdout '^These limits make the layer rules stricter:$'
stdout '^- ConfigUtility can call only: nothing$'
stdout '^- PricingEngine can be called only by: OrderManager, manager$'
! stdout 'OrderManager can'

# Without a .specval.yaml, context exits 2.
cd $WORK
exits 2 sv context
stderr '^sv: no .specval.yaml in '

-- empty/.specval.yaml --
-- limits/.specval.yaml --
-- limits/.specval/data/services.yaml --
services:
  OrderManager: { layer: manager }
  PricingEngine: { layer: engine, called_by: [OrderManager, manager] }
  ConfigUtility: { layer: utility, may_call: [] }
-- numberlayer/.specval.yaml --
-- numberlayer/.specval/data/services.yaml --
services:
  OrderManager: { layer: manager }
  NumberEngine: { layer: 7 }
  StringAccessor: accessor
-- refund.txt --
# Spec rules (specval)

Each change needs a spec at specs/<id>.yaml, and the spec must pass before you write code. To make a new spec, run `sv new <id>`. To check a spec, run `sv check <file>`. Each finding has a fix.

## Format
```yaml
specval: 1
id: ORD-142                # the ticket ID; name the spec file <id>.yaml
kind: feature              # feature, bugfix, or chore
status: draft              # draft; set done in the change that implements the spec
title: Customer can request a refund
summary: One paragraph that tells the problem and the new behavior.
acceptance:                # criteria that you can test, with the IDs AC1, AC2, ...
  - { id: AC1, text: "Given a delivered order, when the customer requests a refund, then ..." }
services:
  new:                     # the services that this change adds
    - { name: RefundEngine, layer: engine, purpose: Calculates refund amounts }
  changed: [OrderManager]  # the registered services whose behavior changes
calls:                     # each call between services that the change adds or changes
  - { from: OrderManager, to: RefundEngine, op: CalculateRefund, covers: [AC1] }
out_of_scope: [Partial refunds]
```

## Layers
Each layer can call only the layers after its arrow.
- client -> manager, utility
- manager -> engine, accessor, utility
- engine -> accessor, utility
- accessor -> resource, utility
- resource -> utility
- utility -> utility
The name of a service ends with the suffix of its layer: Manager, Engine, Accessor, Resource.
A service in these layers has no suffix: client, utility.

## Registered services
- manager: OrderManager
- engine: PricingEngine
- accessor: CatalogAccessor, OrderAccessor

## Rules
- ORD001 (error) Only OrderManager calls PricingEngine: One service controls pricing, so that all discounts apply in the same way.
- SV001 (error) The spec matches the schema: The spec has the fields, the field names, and the value types that the schema (`sv schema`) gives. The other rules run only after this rule passes.
- SV101 (error) Calls use known services: The `from` and the `to` of each call are registered services or services in `services.new`.
- SV102 (error) Calls follow the layer rules: A service can call only the layers that its layer can call (see Layers).
- SV103 (error) Each new service has the suffix of its layer: The name of a new service ends with the suffix of its layer (Manager, Engine, Accessor, or Resource). A client or a utility has no suffix.
- SV104 (error) A new service is not in the registry: A service that is in the registry goes under `services.changed`, not under `services.new`.
- SV105 (error) New services use a known layer: The `layer` of a new service is one of the layers.
- SV106 (error) Each new service is in a call: Each service in `services.new` is in one or more calls.
- SV107 (error) A call implements each acceptance criterion: The ID of each acceptance criterion is in the `covers` list of one or more calls. Thus each acceptance criterion shows the services that implement it. In a spec with no calls, the services in `services.changed` cover each criterion.
- SV108 (error) Each call covers acceptance criteria of the spec: Each ID in the `covers` list of a call is an acceptance criterion of the spec.
- SV109 (error) Each changed service is in the registry: Each name in `services.changed` is in the registry.
- SV110 (error) Each new service has a different name: Each name in `services.new` is there one time only.
- SV111 (error) Calls follow the limits in the registry: A registry entry can limit the calls of a service with `may_call`, and the callers of a service with `called_by`. Each value is a layer or a service. A limit makes the layer rules stricter. It cannot permit more calls.
- SV112 (error) Each removed call is in the registry: Each call in `removed_calls` is in the `calls` of its service in the registry.
- SV113 (error) Each moved service is in the registry: Each `name` in `services.relayered` is a registered service, with its name today.
- SV114 (error) A moved service has the suffix of its new layer: The name of a moved service ends with the suffix of its new layer. If the name today does not, `rename` gives the new name.
- SV115 (error) Moved services use a known layer: The `layer` of each entry in `services.relayered` is one of the layers.
- SV116 (error) A service moves one time only: Each name in `services.relayered` is there one time only.
- SV117 (error) The new name of a moved service is free: The `rename` of a moved service is not the name of a registered service, a new service, a layer, or the new name of another moved service.
- SV118 (error) A spec with no calls names the services that it changes: A change inside one service adds no call between services. Such a spec has `calls: []`, and it puts the service in `services.changed`. The services in `services.changed` then cover each acceptance criterion.
- SV201 (warning) Changed services take part in a call: In a spec with calls, each service in `services.changed` is in one or more calls.
- SV202 (warning) A changed service keeps only calls that the rules permit: The registry records the calls of each service today. For each service in `services.changed`, each call that the spec does not remove follows the layer rules and the limits. Thus a change does not keep a call that breaks the layers.
- SV203 (warning) A moved service keeps only calls that the rules permit: For each service in `services.relayered`, each registered call to or from it that the spec does not remove follows the layer rules and the limits after the move.
